def rcurry(func, n=EMPTY):
    if n is EMPTY:
        _, n, _ = get_spec(func)
    if n <= 1:
        return func
    elif n == 2:
        return lambda x: lambda y: func(y, x)
    else:
        return lambda x: rcurry(rpartial(func, x), n - 1)
```

This creates a chain of functions that each take one positional argument. `rpartial(func, x)` creates a lambda that appends `x` to the end of positional args. Then the next call appends the next arg to the end, so the first called argument ends up at the far right position. That's "right curry" - arguments are applied from the right.

